The Sharp Directions Are Against You: Curvature Analysis of Activation Steering Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.16084v1 [cs.AI] 12 Oct 2026 The Sharp Directions Are Against You: Curvature Analysis of Activation Steering Seyedarmin Azizi † † thanks: Corresponding author: seyedarm@usc.edu . Affiliation: University of Southern California, Los Angeles, USA Arya Fayyazi Affiliation: University of Southern California, Los Angeles, USA Parsa Razmara Affiliation: University of Southern California, Los Angeles, USA Erfan Baghaei Potraghloo Affiliation: University of Southern California, Los Angeles, USA Souvik Kundu Affiliation: Intel AI, USA Massoud Pedram Affiliation: University of Southern California, Los Angeles, USA Abstract Activation steering adds a vector to a language model’s internal representations at inference time. It is a lightweight alternative to fine-tuning for behavioral control. The standard construction, contrastive activation addition (CAA), is fragile, as on many inputs it shifts behavior in the wrong direction. To explain this fragility, we first study the geometry of the steering vector. We introduce steering Hessian , the matrix of second order derivative of the model’s behavioral loss with respect to the steering vector . It captures the output behavior response sensitivity of the model under small perturbations of the steering vector along each direction. Its top eigenvectors are sharp directions : tiny perturbations along them can cause large, erratic, input-dependent behavioral swings. Bottom eigenvectors are flat directions : behavior is robust along them. Removing the sharp component of a steering vector approximately preserves its direction and magnitude (cosine > 0.974 >0.974 , norm reduction < 3 % <3\% for typical settings) while reducing the vector’s directional curvature by up to 12 × 	imes (median 4 × 	imes across 36 conditions). We evaluate on three model families (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B), with four behavioral tasks, and 36 token-level conditions. Steering along sharp directions reverses the intended behavior in every condition tested, with anti-steer rates of 53–100%. Sharp directions account for only 1–5% of the CAA vector’s squared norm. However, they drive a disproportionate share of the gap between the steered and unsteered next-token distributions during autoregressive generation. We term this one-line correction as sharp removal ; it outperforms standard CAA in open-ended generation across all 25 conditions tested. A jailbreaking experiment further shows that sharp directions act as noise rather than signal: they disrupt coherent behavioral control regardless of steering polarity. Content Warning: This paper contains examples of harmful model outputs (Appendices K and M ), included solely to illustrate safety-relevant findings. 1 Introduction Large language models (LLMs) generate fluent text across many domains, but controlling their behavior remains a central challenge. Activation steering ( Turner et al., 2023 ; Zou et al., 2023a ) is a training-free approach to behavioral control. A steering vector 𝐯 ∈ ℝ d \mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} is added to the model’s internal activations at a chosen layer during the forward pass, inducing targeted behavioral changes (e.g., increased honesty or reduced toxicity) without modifying any model parameters. The standard recipe for building a steering vector is contrastive activation addition (CAA) ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ) , which takes the mean activation difference between positive and negative behavioral exemplars. CAA is simple but fragile in well-documented ways: • Anti-steering. On 30–50% of inputs, the CAA vector shifts behavior in the opposite direction from intended ( Tan et al., 2024 ) . • Coefficient sensitivity. Effectiveness depends on the scaling coefficient α \alpha , with a narrow effective range before behavior degrades ( Braun et al., 2025 ) . • Behavioral inversion. As we show in § 5.3 , on three of four tested tasks, the CAA vector has the wrong sign at most layers, steering the model toward the opposite of the intended behavior. These failures are typically treated as empirical nuisances. We give a geometric explanation for why they occur and a low-cost method to fix them. Key insight. Steering vectors live in a high-dimensional activation space, but not all directions in that space behave the same way. Some are catastrophically sensitive to perturbation. Most are not. CAA’s fragility comes from this anisotropy. The mean-difference construction does not distinguish the catastrophic directions from the stable ones, so a small fraction of the CAA vector’s squared norm (typically 1–5%, the sharp energy fraction ε \varepsilon ) lies along the catastrophic directions. That small fraction drives most of CAA’s misbehavior at generation time. Identifying these directions and removing them turns a fragile vector into a robust one, with no retraining and no change to the downstream model. Contributions. The steering Hessian, with theoretical grounding (§ 3 , § 4 ). We introduce the steering Hessian 𝐇 steer \mathbf{H}_{	ext{steer}} , the curvature of the behavioral loss with respect to the steering vector itself (not the model parameters). For each direction in activation space, 𝐇 steer \mathbf{H}_{	ext{steer}} measures how sensitively the model’s behavior responds to perturbing the steering vector along that direction. Two propositions ground the framework. Proposition 1 gives a Gauss-Newton decomposition that explains why top eigenvectors are catastrophic: their effect on logits is governed by the model’s downstream Jacobian, which varies erratically across inputs. Proposition 2 bounds the price of removing them. Removing the top- k k component approximately preserves the steering vector’s direction and magnitude in any single forward pass. At the same time, it reduces the vector’s directional curvature by a factor we denote κ gap \kappa_{	ext{gap}} (1.7–12 × 	imes , median 4 × 	imes in our experiments; Appendix F ). Two empirical findings about steering geometry (§ 5.2 , § 5.3 ). The top eigenvectors of 𝐇 steer \mathbf{H}_{	ext{steer}} are adversarial directions at inference: projecting CAA onto them reverses behavior in all 36 tested conditions (3 models × 	imes 4 tasks × 	imes 3 layers), with anti-steer rates of 53–100%. On refusal, this means that the model’s own curvature exposes directions that undermine safety alignment, without requiring adversarial optimization. The bottom eigenvectors play the complementary role: projecting CAA onto the flat subspace diagnoses when CAA has the wrong sign (3 of 4 tasks) and identifies the task-specific layer at which steering is most effective. Sharp removal: a drop-in correction for CAA (§ 3.3 , § 5.4 ). We turn curvature analysis into a one-line modification of any existing CAA vector: subtract its sharp component to produce a cleaned vector 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} . This drop-in correction outperforms standard CAA in open-ended generation across 25 of 25 tested conditions spanning three tasks and three model families. The same procedure applies to any activation-space steering vector, not just CAA. Crucially, sharp removal is a post-processing wrapper, not a competitor: it composes with existing methods (we demonstrate on Arditi et al. (2024) ’s refusal direction in § 5.6 ). 2 Related Works Activation steering. Concept activation vectors ( Kim et al., 2018 ) and latent steering ( Subramani et al., 2022 ) laid the groundwork for activation-space interventions. Zou et al. (2023a) formalized representation engineering. Turner et al. (2023) introduced Activation Addition, and Rimsky et al. (2024) extended it to CAA. Subsequent work improved steering via adaptive interventions ( Wang et al., 2025 ; Tian et al., 2025 ) , SAE-guided features ( Chalnev et al., 2024 ) , conceptors ( Postmus and Abreu, 2024 ) , gradient optimization ( Dunefsky and Cohan, 2025 ) , and preference-optimized vectors ( Cao et al., 2024 ) . Arditi et al. (2024) identified a refusal direction that mediates safety behavior. We complement this line by giving a geometric account of why steering vectors succeed or fail and by deriving a corresponding correction. Robustness of steering. Tan et al. (2024) documented anti-steering; Braun et al. (2025) showed sensitivity to layer and coefficient. We give a geometric explanation: these failures arise from sharp curvature in the activation-space loss landscape. Sharpness-aware minimization. SAM ( Foret et al., 2021 ) seeks flat minima in weight space; the idea has been extended to LoRA ( Li et al., 2024 ) , unlearning ( Fan et al., 2025 ) , and safety alignment ( Yang et al., 2026 ) . We bring the sharpness concept to the activation space , a different setting. Here, the loss landscape varies with each input, and the parameters of interest are a single d d -dimensional vector rather than billions of model parameters. Jailbreaking and safety alignment. Adversarial attacks on safety have been studied via prompt optimization ( Zou et al., 2023b ) and fine-tuning ( Qi et al., 2025 ) . Our Finding 2 reveals that the steering Hessian identifies directions that reverse safety-aligned behavior with no adversarial optimization. The model’s own curvature structure exposes its safety vulnerabilities. 3 Method 3.1 Setup and Notation Let f θ f_{	heta} denote a frozen language model with parameters θ 	heta . At layer ℓ \ell , the hidden state for input x x is 𝐡 ℓ ​ ( x ) ∈ ℝ d \mathbf{h}_{\ell}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , where d d is the hidden dimension. A steering vector 𝐯 ∈ ℝ d \mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} is injected at layer ℓ \ell with a scalar steering coefficient α > 0 \alpha>0 , modifying the hidden state to: 𝐡 ~ ℓ ​ ( x ) = 𝐡 ℓ ​ ( x ) + α ​ 𝐯 . 	ilde{\mathbf{h}}_{\ell}(x)=\mathbf{h}_{\ell}(x)+\alpha\math