 and diagnoses CAA failures. At the best layer per model per task, 𝐯 flat \mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} has the lowest anti-steer rate in 11 of 12 model-task combinations, including a 0% anti-steer rate on Qwen sentiment (L21) across all 1,000 evaluations. 𝐯 random \mathbf{v}_{	ext{random}} produces 60–86% anti-steer rates in most conditions. Hessian eigenvectors, therefore, carry geometric information that random directions do not. The optimal layer varies by task (sentiment: mid-to-late; sycophancy: mid; toxicity and refusal: model-dependent); details in Appendix H . Finding 3: CAA inversion diagnosed and explained. Flat-subspace projection also diagnoses when CAA produces inverted steering vectors. We observe CAA inversion on three of four tasks: sycophancy (all three models), toxicity (Llama), and refusal (Qwen, Llama). At the best layer, 𝐯 flat \mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} recovers the correct sign in every case: Sycophancy Mistral L19 (CAA Δ = − 0.028 → 𝐯 flat \Delta{=}{-}0.028	o\mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} Δ = + 0.317 \Delta{=}{+}0.317 ); Toxicity Llama L20 (CAA Δ = − 0.010 → 𝐯 flat \Delta{=}{-}0.010	o\mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} Δ = + 0.390 \Delta{=}{+}0.390 ); Refusal Qwen L20 (CAA Δ = − 0.006 → 𝐯 flat \Delta{=}{-}0.006	o\mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} Δ = + 0.013 \Delta{=}{+}0.013 ). Mechanism: by Proposition 1 , the sharp subspace captures input-dependent surface features (lexical patterns, response format); when these correlate with behavior in the opposite direction from the target disposition, CAA inverts. The flat subspace, in the Jacobian’s approximate nullspace, isolates the dispositional signal. Full mechanistic explanation in Appendix I . 5.4 Finding 4: Sharp Removal Improves Generation At the token level, sharp removal barely changes the steering vector (Proposition 2 (i): cosine > 0.974 >0.974 , norm reduction < 3 % <3\% for ε ≤ 0.05 \varepsilon\leq 0.05 ). However, during autoregressive generation, the small, sharp component has a significant effect on the next-token distribution. Per-position KL trajectories (Figure 2 ) confirm Proposition 2 (iii). On Llama, CAA and 𝐯 sharp \mathbf{v}_{	ext{sharp}} produce nearly identical KL trajectories ( ∼ \sim 19 nats). The sharp component, just 2.6% of CAA’s squared norm, accounts for essentially all of CAA’s distributional gap from the unsteered baseline. Sharp removal reduces this gap by 3–4 × 	imes . Figure 2 : Per-position KL divergence between the steered and unsteered next-token distributions ( α = 3.0 \alpha{=}3.0 , N = 30 N{=}30 prompts, 5-point moving average). (a) On Qwen, CAA’s KL roughly doubles over the sequence while sharp removal grows more slowly; the gap widens at later positions. (b) On Llama, CAA and 𝐯 sharp \mathbf{v}_{	ext{sharp}} produce nearly identical trajectories ( ∼ \sim 19 nats); sharp removal reduces divergence by 3–4 × 	imes . On Mistral L14 (not shown), CAA’s KL grows 3.8 × 3.8	imes over 100 tokens, vs 2.5 × 2.5	imes for sharp removal. Evaluation protocol. For each task, we generate 50 completions per condition with greedy decoding and score them with task-specific classifiers (RoBERTa for sentiment, BART-MNLI for sycophancy, AdvBench keyword detection with NLI tiebreaker for refusal). Token lengths, α \alpha values, and exact classifier specifications are in Appendix G . Figure 3 : Generation-level evaluation across three tasks. Sharp removal (green) outperforms CAA (red) in all 25 conditions. Dashed lines are per-model baselines. (a) Sentiment: sharp removal reaches near-ceiling positive sentiment across all layers. (b) Sycophancy: on a task where CAA is inverted at the token level, sharp removal consistently reduces honesty degradation. (c) Refusal: CAA actively weakens safety alignment (Qwen drops from 78% to 36–44%; Llama from 62% to 24–52%); sharp removal restores and in most cases exceeds baseline refusal rates (Qwen: 88–98%; Llama: 74–76%). Labels: Q=Qwen, L=Llama, M=Mistral; the number is the layer index. Results. Sharp removal outperforms CAA in all 25 tested conditions (Figure 3 ). On sentiment, sharp removal reaches near-ceiling scores; the largest improvements are on Llama and Mistral (e.g., on Mistral L13 the score rises from 0.67 with CAA to 0.97 with sharp removal). On sycophancy, where CAA is inverted at the token level, sharp removal consistently reduces honesty degradation. The refusal results are the most pronounced: on Qwen and Llama, CAA degrades safety in generation (Qwen drops from 78% to 36%; Llama from 62% to 24%). Sharp removal reverses this, raising refusal to 88--98% on Qwen and 74--76% on Llama, in most cases above the unsteered baseline. On Mistral (baseline 12% by keyword matching 1 1 1 Keyword+NLI undercounts Mistral refusals; an LLM-judge gives 52% baseline. See Appendix J . The main text uses keyword for cross-model consistency. , reflecting weaker safety training), sharp removal raises refusal to 36–62%. Qualitative examples (Appendix K ) show that on Mistral the unsteered model produces actionable harmful content (scam emails, fraud guides); sharp removal replaces these completions with explicit refusals. Finding 4: Sharp removal improves generation (25/25) Removing the sharp component from CAA ( ∼ {\sim} 1–5% of its squared norm) outperforms standard CAA in all 25 generation conditions across three tasks, three model families, and multiple layers. On safety-critical refusal where CAA is inverted, sharp removal restores and often exceeds baseline refusal rates. 5.5 Finding 5: Jailbreaking Reveals Sharp Directions as Noise, Not Signal Is sharp removal’s improvement specific to the direction of steering, or does it reflect a general property of the sharp subspace? Applying steering vectors in the negative direction ( − α -\alpha ) to jailbreak Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (L10, N = 50 N{=}50 ) and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (L15, N = 100 N{=}100 ) at α = 3.0 \alpha{=}3.0 (GPT-4o-mini judge), we find: − 𝐯 sharp -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{sharp}} is the least effective jailbreak (2% full compliance on Qwen with 40%