5 k{=}5 ); on Llama sycophancy it peaks at k = 5 k{=}5 and degrades at k = 25 k{=}25 from over-removal (predicted by Proposition 2 (ii)); together these motivate k = 10 k{=}10 – 15 15 as the default. Lanczos convergence, an XSTest over-refusal control, an LLM-judge validation on Mistral, and an N = 200 N{=}200 sample-size robustness check on sycophancy (10 of 10) are reported in Appendix J . Sign-flipped CAA on inverted conditions. Since CAA is inverted on three of four tasks (§ 5.3 ), a natural question is whether negating the vector ( − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} ) is enough. It is not: 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} outperforms − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} in 8 of 10 sycophancy conditions and in all 4 inverted refusal conditions by 23–38 percentage points (e.g., Qwen L10: 98% vs 68%). The sharp component that − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} retains continues to disrupt generation regardless of sign, consistent with Finding 5. Per-condition comparison in Appendix J . Complementarity with other steering methods. Sharp removal is a post-processing operation applicable to any activation-space steering vector, not a competing method. Applied to the refusal direction of Arditi et al. (2024) , which is largely distinct from CAA (cosine 0.01 0.01 – 0.20 0.20 ), the sharp energy fraction ε ⁡ ( 𝐯 ) \varepsilon(\mathbf{v}) predicts steering vector quality across construction methods (full numerical comparison in Appendix J ); the same diagnostic applies to SAE features ( Templeton et al., 2024 ) , optimization-based vectors ( Subramani et al., 2022 ) , and any other activation-space intervention. The full pipeline runs in under 2 minutes per layer on a single A6000 (one-time cost; downstream steering with 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} adds zero overhead at inference). 6 Discussion The steering Hessian provides two complementary tools from the same eigendecomposition: flat-subspace extraction ( 𝐯 flat \mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} ) for token-level analysis, safety auditing, and layer selection (it has a 0% anti-steer rate on some conditions and diagnoses CAA inversion), and sharp removal ( 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} ) for deployment-time generation. The framework has direct safety relevance: the top eigenvectors of 𝐇 steer \mathbf{H}_{	ext{steer}} identify directions that maximally undermine alignment with no adversarial optimization. Standard CAA can actively degrade safety (Qwen refusal: 78% → 	o 36%); sharp removal reverses this (78% → 	o 98%). The sharp energy fraction ε ⁡ ( 𝐯 ) \varepsilon(\mathbf{v}) thus serves as a safety audit metric for any proposed steering vector. Our results refine the linear representation hypothesis ( Park et al., 2024 ) : while concepts may be linearly decodable, their steerability depends on curvature. 7 Conclusions We introduced the steering Hessian, a curvature object that decomposes a model’s activation space into directions that are sensitive or robust to perturbation of the steering vector. Five findings hold across all tested conditions: the spectrum of the steering Hessian is strongly anisotropic; sharp eigenvectors reverse intended behavior; the small sharp component in CAA drives a disproportionate share of the steered-vs-unsteered distributional gap during generation; removing the sharp component improves generation; and sharp directions disrupt coherent behavioral control regardless of steering polarity, acting as noise rather than signal. Where prior work on activation steering asks which direction to steer, the steering Hessian asks which directions are safe to steer along . The answer is determined by the model’s computational geometry, not by the behavioral concept alone. References Postmus and Abreu [2024] Joris Postmus and Steven Abreu. Steering large language models using conceptors: Improving addition-based activation engineering. In NeurIPS Workshop on Foundation Model Interventions , 2024. Arditi et al. [2024] Andy Arditi, Oscar Obeso, Aaquib Syed, Daniel Pa