pace has principal-angle cosine of 0.61 0.61 to the single-token subspace. The 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} produced under the multi-token objective performs comparably to the single-token one (matched- N N honesty 0.83 0.83 vs 0.85 0.85 ). Proposition 1 explains this robustness: the curvature structure is dominated by the model’s downstream Jacobian, which is a property of the model’s computation graph and is shared across behavioral objectives. The framework therefore generalizes to any differentiable behavioral objective, including multi-token losses or reward-model scores. Appendix B Method Extras: Sharp Removal Norm Properties and Flat Subspace Norm properties of 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} . We do not renormalize 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} after sharp removal, so its norm is slightly lower than ‖ 𝐯 CAA ‖ \|\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}}\| (typically 95–99% of it, since the sharp component carries 1–5% of CAA’s squared norm). Why removal rather than flat extraction. An alternative is to project CAA onto the flat subspace ℱ k \mathcal{F}_{k} , yielding 𝐯 flat = 𝐏 ℱ k ​ 𝐯 CAA \mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}}=\mathbf{P}_{\mathcal{F}_{k}}\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} (renormalized to the original norm). The flat-subspace projection has a 0% anti-steer rate at the token level on some conditions and is invaluable for diagnosing CAA inversion (§ 5.3 ). However, 𝐯 flat \mathbf{v}_{	ext{flat}} discards all signals outside the bottom- k k eigenvectors, which limits its strength in open-ended generation. Sharp removal is a better practical method because it preserves the full CAA signal while removing only the ∼ {\sim} 1–5% of energy that dominates the distributional perturbation during generation (§ 5.4 ). Appendix C Algorithm Pseudocode Algorithm 1 Hessian-Informed Sharp Removal 0: Frozen model f θ f_{	heta} , layer ℓ \ell , N N contrastive pairs { ( x i + , x i − ) } i = 1 N \{(x_{i}^{+},x_{i}^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N} , estimation prompts { ( x j , y j ) } \{(x_{j},y_{j})\}