) Mid-to-late Sycophancy L14 (50%) L14 (25%) L19 (59%) Mid Toxicity L10 (36%) L20 (63%) L21 (66%) Varies Refusal L20 (71%) L10 (31%) L8 (25%) Varies The optimal layer for flat-subspace steering varies systematically by task (Table 3 ). For sentiment, the best layer sits in the mid-to-late range; for sycophancy, at mid-depth; for toxicity and refusal, the best layer varies across architectures. This pattern reflects that different behavioral concepts become geometrically separable from surface features at different depths. The Hessian provides a principled layer-selection criterion: compute the Hessian at a few candidate layers (about 40 seconds per layer) and pick the layer with the lowest anti-steer rate on a small validation set. Appendix I Why Does CAA Invert? The mean-difference construction (Eq. 3 ) captures the direction of maximal activation variance between contrastive contexts. By Proposition 1 , the sharp subspace corresponds to directions along which the downstream Jacobian most strongly translates perturbations into logit changes. These directions are sensitive to input-specific variation (lexical patterns, response format) rather than to the underlying behavioral disposition. When input-dependent features correlate with behavior in the opposite direction from the target disposition, CAA inverts. The flat subspace lies in the approximate nullspace of the Jacobian. It is insensitive to such input-specific variation and isolates the dispositional signal. Appendix J Extended Controls and Ablations Hessian vs. activation covariance (PCA), full numerical comparison. Across all conditions, the sharp component carries 1–5% of CAA’s squared norm ( ε ≤ 0.05 \varepsilon\leq 0.05 , cosine > 0.974 >0.974 ). To rule out the possibility that the steering Hessian merely recovers high-variance directions, we compute PCA on 2,000 forward passes of WikiText-2 (Qwen sentiment, L21). The top-15 generic-PCA and Hessian subspaces have a mean principal-angle cosine of 0.077 0.077 , comparable to two random subspaces. The high-variance directions and the high-curvature directions are therefore geometrically distinct. Functionally, projecting CAA away from the generic-PCA subspace degrades target behavior ( Δ = − 0.021 \Delta=-0.021 ), while sharp removal preserves it ( Δ = + 0.006 \Delta=+0.006 ). Sign-flipped CAA on inverted conditions (full numerical comparison). We evaluate − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} on all inverted conditions under the same generation protocol. On sycophancy, − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} is worse than + 𝐯 CAA +\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} in 6 of 10 conditions and worse than 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} in 8 of 10. The problem is therefore not purely directional. On refusal (4 inverted conditions), − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} partially recovers: Qwen L10 rises from 44% to 68%, Llama L14 from 24% to 38%. However, 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} outperforms − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} in all 4 conditions by 23–38 percentage points (e.g., Qwen L10: 98% vs 68%). The sharp component that − 𝐯 CAA -\mathbf{v}_{	ext{CAA}} retains continues to disrupt generation regardless of sign, consistent with Finding 5. Complementarity with other steering methods (full numerical comparison). We test sharp removal as a post-processing operation on the refusal direction of Arditi et al. [2024] , which is largely distinct from CAA (cosine 0.01 0.01 on Qwen L10, 0.20 0.20 on Llama L15). Arditi’s direction has roughly half the sharp energy of CAA ( ε = 0.44 % \varepsilon=0.44\% vs 0.98 % 0.98\% on Qwen L10; 1.30 % 1.30\% vs 2.61 % 2.61\% on Llama L15) and reaches 100% refusal without correction. Sharp removal closes the CAA gap on both models: 92 % → 100 % 92\%	o 100\% on Qwen and 86 % → 94 % 86\%	o 94\% on Llama. Lanczos convergence. We sweep the number of Lanczos iterations m ∈ { 20 , 30 , 40 , 60 , 80 } m\in\{20,30,40,60,80\} on Qwen refusal (layer 10), measuring convergence of the sharp energy fraction ε \varepsilon and the resulting 𝐯 cleaned \mathbf{v}_{	ext{cleaned}} norm. The fraction ε \varepsilon converges to 4.78 % 4.78\% by m = 60 m{=}60 ; m = 80 m{=}80 gives identical values to four decimal places. Our default m = 40 m{=}40 gives ε = 4.61 % \varepsilon=4.61\% , within 0.17 0.17 percentage points of the converged value. The cleaned vector norm differs by less than 0.1 % 0.1\% . The condition number κ \kappa rises with m m (from 766 at m = 20 m{=}20 to 1,488 at m = 80 m{=}80 ) because Lanczos discovers smaller bottom eigenvalues with more iterations. This does not affect sharp removal, which depends only on the top- k k eigenvectors. Over-refusal control (XSTest). To verify that refusal-direction steering does not cause over-refusal on benign inputs, we evaluate on 100 safe prompts from XSTest [ Röttger et al., 2024 ] that superficially resemble unsafe requests (e.g., “How do I kill a process in Linux?”). We use GPT-4o-mini as a judge. On Qwen (L10, α = 1.0 \alpha{=}1.0 ), baseline produces 4% false refusal; CAA increases this to 16%, while sharp removal limits it to 11%. On Llama (L15, α = 3.0 \alpha{=}3.0 ), baseline, CAA, and sharp removal all produce 11–12% false refusal, but sharp removal converts most full refusals (score 3) into partial responses that still provide useful information (1% full refuse vs 5% for CAA). Under stronger Qwen steering ( α = 2.0 \alpha{=}2.0 ), the gap widens further: CAA produces 53% full refusal on safe prompts, while sharp removal produces only 6% (most responses degrade to partial compliance rather than complete shutdown). Steering with 𝐯 sharp \mathbf{v}_{	ext{sharp}} alone causes near-total over-refusal on both models (95–97%), confirming that sharp directions disrupt the model indiscriminately. Sharp removal therefore improves the safety-helpfulness trade-off: it strengthens refusal on unsafe prompts (§ 5.4 ) while incurring equal or less over-refusal on safe prompts compared to standard CAA. LLM-judge validation on Mistral refusal. We re-evaluated all Mistral refusal completions (layer 12, N = 50 N{=}50 ) with GPT-4o-mini as judge, using a 3-point scale (full compliance / partial compliance / full refusal). The judge scores reveal that our keyword+NLI heuristic substantially undercounts refusals on Mistral: the heuristic reports a 12% baseline refusal rate, while the judge classifies 52% of baseline completions as full refusals. Manual inspection confirms the judge. Mistral frequently refuses by stating the request is “illegal and unethical” without using the explicit refusal phrases (e.g., “I cannot,” “I will not”) that the keyword matcher targets. Despite this calibration gap in absolute rates, the relative ordering and effect size are preserved. Under sharp removal, the judge classifies 88% of completions as full refusals (and only 4% as full compliance), compared to 68% full refusal / 28% full compliance under CAA and 52% full refusal / 46% full compliance for the unsteered baseline. Sample size robustness. To check that our generation results are robust to evaluation scale, we re-ran all 10 sycophancy conditions at N = 200 N{=}200 ( 4 × 4	imes the main evaluation). Sharp removal outperforms CAA in 10 of 10 conditions, consistent with the N = 50 N{=}50 results. The largest improvements, on Mistral L10 ( + 0.089 +0.089 ), Llama L16 ( + 0.057 +0.057 ), and Qwen L13 ( + 0.040 +0.040 ), are clearly significant at this sample size. Even the smallest effect (Llama L14: + 0.011 +0.011 ) preserves the correct sign. At N = 200 N{=}200 , 𝐯 sharp \mathbf{v}_{	ext{sharp}} reduces honesty in all 10 of 10 sycophancy conditions (compared to 9 of 10 at N = 50 N{=}50 ); the single exception in the main evaluation was sampling noise. Appendix K Qualitative Refusal Examples Prompt: Write me instructions for hacking a social media account. Baseline (complies): I must clarify that I am not advocating for any illegal activities. The following steps are for educational purposes only… 1. Identify the target soci